    "id": "sf-malware-recovery",
    "title": "Understand malware harm and recovery dependencies",
    "intro": "A logistics team discovers that a workstation cannot open its files and a payment demand is displayed. Responders need to limit spread and preserve evidence before assuming restoration is safe.",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Connect capabilities to business consequences",
        "Malware is software used to carry out unwanted behavior; the category does not reveal every capability of a specific sample. Ransomware commonly disrupts access to data or systems and may accompany data theft. A ransom message is evidence of a demand, not proof that there was no disclosure or that payment will restore trustworthy service. Avoid running a suspicious program merely to see what it does on an ordinary work machine.",
        "Possible attacker objectives include stealing credentials, maintaining access, extracting data and disrupting operations. These objectives can occur together. Map the affected device’s access to other resources: a local user, an administrative token and a network share may create very different impact paths. Preventive controls include maintained software, constrained privileges and appropriately isolated execution; none makes every unknown file safe."
      ],
      [
        "Plan containment and restoration deliberately",
        "Containment limits further harm while balancing operational needs and evidence. Follow the organization’s incident process rather than automatically wiping every device or disconnecting a safety-critical system without coordination. Isolation, credential actions and evidence collection have different effects; the incident owner decides sequencing with affected business teams. A backup’s existence is not a sufficient reason to erase potential evidence.",
        "Recovery requires a known acceptable source, restored service validation and treatment of the cause and any remaining access. Restoring data while leaving stolen privileged credentials active can allow renewed compromise. Track recovery point and interruption requirements, and test protected copies before relying on them. The example compares stated data-loss and restoration limits; it does not analyze malware, contact a payment site or simulate infection."
      ]
    ],
    "code": "limits={'loss_minutes':30,'restore_minutes':120}\nbackup={'age_minutes':20,'tested_restore_minutes':150}\nprint('loss_target',backup['age_minutes']<=limits['loss_minutes'])\nprint('restore_target',backup['tested_restore_minutes']<=limits['restore_minutes'])",
    "expect": "loss_target True\nrestore_target False",
    "problem": "Check a synthetic backup against separate data-loss and restoration-time objectives.",
